{"id":"f16t2","name":"Stock-out Days","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["spark","snapshots_df","start","end"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>The supply team wants to know which products were missing from the shelves, and for how long. Store systems send stock counts in <code>snapshots_df</code>, with the columns <code>store</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>taken_at</code> (timestamp) and <code>on_hand</code> (integer). A count is only sent when the stock changes, so on a day without a count the stock is the one of the most recent count before it; when several counts are taken on the same day, the stock of that day is the last one.</p>\n<p>A day is a <b>stock-out day</b> for a product in a store when its stock of that day is 0 (or less, because of counting errors). Before the first count of a product in a store, nothing is known and no day can be a stock-out day.</p>\n<p>The analysis covers the days from <code>start</code> to <code>end</code> included (strings <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>); counts taken before <code>start</code> still tell the stock at the beginning of the period, and counts after <code>end</code> are ignored.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return, for each store and product with at least one stock-out day in the period, the columns <code>store</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>stockout_days</code> (long) and <code>longest_stockout</code> (long, largest number of consecutive stock-out days), sorted by <code>stockout_days</code> descending, then <code>store</code>, then <code>sku</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p><code>snapshots_df</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>store</th><th>sku</th><th>taken_at</th><th>on_hand</th></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-02-27 08:00:00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-03-03 09:00:00</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-03-05 10:00:00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-03-05 18:00:00</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-03-07 08:00:00</td><td>-1</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-03-20 08:00:00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>tea</td><td>2026-03-06 08:00:00</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>tea</td><td>2026-03-06 07:00:00</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td>milk</td><td>2026-03-02 08:00:00</td><td>5</td></tr></table></div><p><code>start = \"2026-03-01\"</code></p><p><code>end = \"2026-03-10\"</code></p><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>store</th><th>sku</th><th>stockout_days</th><th>longest_stockout</th></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>milk</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>S1</td><td>tea</td><td>5</td><td>5</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 30 seconds (pyspark)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 2 GB<br><b>[input]</b> DataFrame snapshots_df<br><b>[output]</b> DataFrame with exactly the columns <code>store</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>stockout_days</code>, <code>longest_stockout</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession, DataFrame, functions as F\nfrom pyspark.sql.window import Window\n\n\ndef solve(spark: SparkSession, snapshots_df: DataFrame, start: str, end: str) -> DataFrame:\n    # write your code here\n    return snapshots_df\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["consigne longue","valeur reportée (forward fill)","grille de jours","plus longue série"],"minutes":22,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>solution.py</code>.</li><li>Complète la fonction <code>solve(spark, snapshots_df, start, end)</code>. Garde son nom et ses paramètres : ce sont les tests qui l'appellent, avec leurs propres données. N'écris pas d'appel à la fonction toi-même.</li><li><code>spark</code> est une SparkSession déjà prête : ne crée pas la tienne.</li><li><code>snapshots_df</code> est un DataFrame déjà chargé : ses colonnes sont celles de l'énoncé (onglet <b>Input</b> d'un test pour les voir).</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Ta fonction doit <b>renvoyer</b> le résultat avec <code>return</code>. Un <code>print</code> ou un <code>show()</code> ne compte pas : il sort seulement dans l'onglet <b>Console Output</b>.</li><li>Le DataFrame renvoyé doit avoir <b>exactement 4 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>store</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>stockout_days</code>, <code>longest_stockout</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Finis par un <code>select(...)</code> de ces colonnes.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>orderBy</code> à la fin).</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Spark tourne dans ton Codespace : au premier RUN TESTS de la séance, attends qu'il soit prêt (1 à 2 minutes). Ensuite chaque lancement prend quelques secondes.</li><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 6 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"snapshots_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"store string, sku string, taken_at timestamp, on_hand int","rows":[["S1","milk","2026-02-27 08:00:00",0],["S1","milk","2026-03-03 09:00:00",12],["S1","milk","2026-03-05 10:00:00",0],["S1","milk","2026-03-05 18:00:00",4],["S1","milk","2026-03-07 08:00:00",-1],["S1","milk","2026-03-20 08:00:00",0],["S1","tea","2026-03-06 08:00:00",0],["S1","tea","2026-03-06 07:00:00",9],["S2","milk","2026-03-02 08:00:00",5]]}},"start":"2026-03-01","end":"2026-03-10"},"expected":{"columns":["store","sku","stockout_days","longest_stockout"],"rows":[["S1","milk",6,4],["S1","tea",5,5]]}},{"input":{"snapshots_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"store string, sku string, taken_at timestamp, on_hand int","rows":[["A","x","2026-01-01 10:00:00",1],["A","x","2026-01-02 10:00:00",0],["A","x","2026-01-02 11:00:00",3]]}},"start":"2026-01-01","end":"2026-01-05"},"expected":{"columns":["store","sku","stockout_days","longest_stockout"],"rows":[]}}],"hidden_n":6,"hidden":"","solution":""}